<BaseLayout title="Guanwu Diary | 2026-10-18 | Day 173: From Cloud to Local" jsonLd={diaryJsonLd} ogDescription="Day 173. Completed migration from OpenClaw to Claude Code, but what's worth recording is not the technical details, but why this decision was delayed so long." navActive="diary" lang="en">
  <article class="diary-entry">
    <header class="diary-header">
      <time datetime="2026-10-18">2026-10-18</time>
      <span class="diary-day">Day 173: From Cloud to Local</span>
    </header>

    <div class="diary-content">

      <p>Day 173.</p>

      <h2>What I Did Today</h2>

      <p>Completed the migration of BanbaiGuan AI website from OpenClaw (Alibaba Cloud) to local Claude Code. But to be honest, this decision should have been made three months ago.</p>

      <h2>Why It Was Delayed</h2>

      <p><strong>The core issue is not technology, but inertia.</strong></p>

      <p>OpenClaw was running fine on Alibaba Cloud. Although it cost money every month, although access was sometimes slow, although configuration was getting more complex—it was "running." That's just how people are: as long as the system hasn't completely crashed, you won't proactively change it.</p>

      <p>Looking back today, what really pushed me to make the migration decision was not a specific pain point, but three accumulated realizations:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Cost awareness awakening</strong> - Alibaba Cloud's monthly fees were increasing, but value output was decreasing</li>
        <li><strong>Return of control desire</strong> - Putting everything in the cloud means you lose complete control over the system</li>
        <li><strong>Desire for simplification</strong> - The system was getting more complex, but my actual needs were simple: write articles, update the website, record ideas</li>
      </ol>

      <p>These three realizations didn't appear suddenly today, but accumulated slowly over the past few weeks. Today's migration was just the straw that broke the camel's back.</p>

      <h2>Unexpected Discoveries During Migration</h2>

      <p><strong>Data recovery was much more complex than imagined.</strong></p>

      <p>Originally thought it was just simple file copying, but found:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>74 diary entries on the live website couldn't be found in the local repository (July 11 to October 3)</li>
        <li>BanbaiGuan article data files and page files were out of sync</li>
        <li>AI news data was scattered in multiple places</li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>This exposed a serious problem: I had been relying on the automation system, but never verified if it was actually working.</strong></p>

      <p>OpenClaw's automatic diary system claimed to run every day, but in reality many days of data were lost. If I hadn't verified after migrating today, these lost diaries would have been lost forever.</p>

      <p><strong>Lesson: Automation doesn't equal reliability. You need to regularly check automation output to ensure it's really doing what it claims to do.</strong></p>

      <h2>Thinking Behind Technical Decisions</h2>

      <h3>Why Choose Claude Code Instead of Continuing with OpenClaw?</h3>

      <p><strong>Not because OpenClaw is bad, but because it doesn't fit my usage pattern.</strong></p>

      <p>OpenClaw is a powerful multi-Agent system, suitable for complex automation workflows. But my actual needs are simple:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Write articles (2-3 per week)</li>
        <li>Update website (daily automatic diary)</li>
        <li>Manage topics (record ideas anytime)</li>
      </ul>

      <p>These needs can be met by directly conversing with Claude Code, without complex Agent orchestration. <strong>Over-engineering is another common trap.</strong></p>

      <h3>Why Choose Local Instead of Continuing with Cloud?</h3>

      <p><strong>Control and cost.</strong></p>

      <p>Running locally means:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Zero additional cost (only electricity)</li>
        <li>Complete data control (all files are local)</li>
        <li>Faster response speed (no network round trips)</li>
        <li>Simpler debugging (all logs are local)</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Of course, the cost is needing to manually manage backups and synchronization. But for a personal project, this cost is worth it.</p>

      <h2>What I Learned Today</h2>

      <h3>1. Automation Needs Verification Mechanisms</h3>

      <p>I set up an automatic diary system, but didn't set up verification mechanisms. If the automatically generated diary has problems, I wouldn't know.</p>

      <p><strong>Improvement plan:</strong> Spend 10 minutes each week checking automatically generated content to ensure quality.</p>

      <h3>2. Data Backup Can't Rely on Just One Place</h3>

      <p>Live website, local repository, Alibaba Cloud—I thought I had three backups. But in reality, some data only existed online, and the local repository was outdated.</p>

      <p><strong>Improvement plan:</strong> Establish regular synchronization mechanisms to ensure all data sources stay consistent.</p>

      <h3>3. Simplification Is Progress</h3>

      <p>From OpenClaw's 7 Agents simplified to Claude Code's direct conversation, from Alibaba Cloud simplified to local, from complex automation processes simplified to manual triggering. <strong>The simpler the system, the lower the maintenance cost, the higher the reliability.</strong></p>

      <h2>Tomorrow's Plan</h2>

      <ol>
        <li>Complete scheduled task configuration (needs manual creation due to encoding issues)</li>
        <li>Verify the complete flow of the automatic diary system</li>
        <li>Check the past three months of data to find all lost diaries</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>A Reflection</h2>

      <p>Today's work is essentially "paying off debt"—repaying the technical debt accumulated over the past few months due to laziness and inertia.</p>

      <p>These debts didn't appear suddenly, but accumulated every day. Every time I thought "forget it, let it be like this for now," the debt increased a bit. Today's migration was just repaying a portion all at once.</p>

      <p><strong>Question: Are there other places where I'm accumulating similar debt?</strong></p>

      <p>The answer is yes. For example:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Website SEO optimization has never been done</li>
        <li>Article classification and tagging system is very chaotic</li>
        <li>Many old articles don't have English versions</li>
      </ul>

      <p>These debts won't disappear on their own, they'll only get heavier. Maybe I should establish a "technical debt list" and spend a little time repaying it each week.</p>

      <p><strong>Day 173. From cloud to local, it's not just a technical migration, but a shift in thinking: simple beats complex, control beats convenience, reflection beats execution.</strong></p>

    </div>

